Title:
SliverMirage — Crystal Palace PICO loader for Sliver C2 (dual-layer AMSI bypass + ETW silencing)

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
SliverMirage is a loader framework that wraps a Sliver implant DLL into a Crystal Palace PICO-virtualized payload and delivers it via multiple formats (EXE/DLL/shellcode; staged or stageless). It focuses on userland evasion by defeating AMSI and suppressing ETW without patching `amsi.dll` or `ntdll.dll`, relying instead on load-time manipulation and hardware breakpoints. The repo documents a two-layer AMSI bypass (CLR string corruption before `GetProcAddress`, plus VEH + HWBP forcing `AmsiScanBuffer` to fail) and ETW silencing via HWBP on `NtTraceControl`. Payloads are encrypted (AES-256-CBC, plus XOR for stageless embedding) and mapped RW→RX to avoid RWX memory. Staged variants fetch an encrypted blob over HTTPS from a Sliver “website” endpoint, while stageless variants embed the encrypted payload to avoid pre-beacon network activity. It’s primarily useful for red teams and offensive security researchers evaluating loader tradecraft and defensive visibility around AMSI/ETW and virtualization-based execution.

Technical Focus:
- Crystal Palace PICO virtualization (running payload in a PICO process)
- AMSI bypass via CLR load interception (`LdrRegisterDllNotification`) and `.rdata` corruption
- Vectored Exception Handler (VEH) + hardware breakpoints (HWBP) on `AmsiScanBuffer`
- ETW suppression via HWBP on `NtTraceControl`
- AES-256-CBC payload encryption/decryption with Windows BCrypt; RW→RX memory permissions
- Staged vs stageless delivery; sRDI DLL-to-shellcode conversion

Use Cases:
- Generate Sliver-compatible loaders in EXE/DLL/shellcode formats for controlled engagements
- Test EDR/AV detections against HWBP/VEH-based AMSI and ETW interference (no DLL patching)
- Evaluate defensive telemetry gaps when payloads execute inside PICO-virtualized contexts
- Build staged HTTPS delivery workflows using Sliver “websites” content hosting
- Research entropy/packing tradeoffs (XOR layer to reduce embedded payload entropy)

Keywords:
Sliver C2, Crystal Palace, PICO, CrystalSliver, loader, AMSI, AmsiScanBuffer, ETW, NtTraceControl, hardware breakpoints, debug registers DR1, VEH, Vectored Exception Handler, LdrRegisterDllNotification, clr.dll, GetProcAddress, BCrypt, AES-256-CBC, VirtualAlloc, VirtualProtect, RW-to-RX, sRDI, reflective DLL injection, staged payload, stageless payload, HTTPS stager, mingw-w64, nasm